dsh: reasoning:
Two issues to address in my verifier (not in the artifact):

1. C1 "order_move" mismatch: stored order_move = |p_base - p_plat| where p values are stored rounded floats (full float64 but computed by numpy polyfit with log(dx)); my pure-Python OLS on log(n) gives a slightly different value at ~1e-9 relative for order2_standing, 1e-11/1e-12 for others. Why the difference? Because numpy polyfit uses a least-squares solve with different numerical conditioning (Vandermonde in log(dx) with intercept) vs my centered OLS — tiny fp differences amplified. TOL_DERIVED=1e-12 relative is too tight for order_move, which is a difference of two nearly equal fit orders (catastrophic cancellation): p_base - p_plat ≈ 2.4e-6 while p ≈ 2, so relative error in p of 1e-15 → absolute 2e-15; but relative error in order_move = 2e-15/2.4e-6 ≈ 1e-9. Exactly what we see (5.5e-10). So my tolerance for order_move must be *absolute*, not relative — e.g. 1e-9 absolute. The right tolerance: |computed - stored| ≤ 1e-9 (absolute). Deviations are 1.7e-12, 5.5e-10, 2.2e-11 — all ≤ 1e-9. Set TOL_ORDER_MOVE = 1e-9 absolute. Also compare p_baseline/p_plateau with TOL_FIT relative 1e-12 — did those pass? Apparently yes (not flagged) — interesting; polyfit vs my OLS agree for p directly to better than 1e-12 relative. Good.

2. Control E2 failed: my tamper set `tam2["runs"][base_key]["fit_order"] = p_baseline+0.06`, but `derive()` ignores the stored fit_order (it recomputes from rows). So the tamper has no effect. The control must tamper the *rows* to move the fitted order, or test the comparison function rather than derive. Better: E2 should verify the *rule* reacts to order move > tol. To do that via derive, I need to alter rows so that the recomputed p_baseline rises by >0.05. Simplest: change the baseline run's l2 error at the *coarsest* n (n=64) to make the fitted slope shallower. For order2_pulse: to raise p_baseline by 0.06, we can scale e at the coarsest point. p = slope; with 5 points equally spaced in log n. Raising the first y by δ changes slope. The slope b = Σ(x-x̄)(y-ȳ)/Sxx. Changing y_0 by Δy changes b by Δy*(x_0-x̄)/Sxx. x = log(n), n = [64,128,256,512,1024]; log spacing d=log2. x_0-x̄ = -2d. Sxx = d²(4+1+0+1+4)=10d². So Δb = Δy*(-2d)/(10d²) = -Δy/(5d). We want |Δb| ≥ 0.06 → Δy = -0.3d ≈ -0.2079 (natural log), i.e. multiply e_0 by exp(-0.3*ln2)=2^-0.3=0.812. But wait, changing e_0 changes excess too and maybe other checks — E2 only needs admissible False. Scaling e_0 down by 0.812 raises p by ~0.06 → order_move > 0.05 → inadmissible. But also it changes the excess_coarsest (e_base[0] lower → excess negative ~ -0.19). Then the rule still rejects (order_move > tol). Fine.

But careful: the direction: y = log(e). fit_order = -slope(log n, log e). Δfit = -Δslope = +Δy_0 * 2d/(10d²) = Δy_0/(5d). Wait let me redo: slope b = Σ(x-x̄)(y-ȳ)/Sxx. Δb from Δy_0 = Δy_0*(x_0-x̄)/Sxx = Δy_0*(-2d)/(10d²) = -Δy_0/(5d). fit_order = -b, so Δfit = Δy_0/(5d). With d=ln2, Δfit = Δy_0/(5*0.6931) = 0.2885*Δy_0. To get Δfit ≥ 0.06: Δy_0 ≥ 0.208 → e_0 multiplied by exp(0.208)=1.231. So scale e_base[0] by 1.25 (Δfit ≈ 0.0644). Also changes excess_coarsest to 0.25*(...)+... admissible still False (order_move 0.064 > 0.05). Good.

Simpler robust alternative: for E2, change two coarsest baseline errors upward. But scaling e_0 by 1.25 moves fit by ~0.064. Let me verify: with 5 points, exact formula. Use factor 1.3 for margin (Δfit ≈ 0.0756).

Actually cleanest: implement E2 by directly testing the rule function with an injected p_baseline (unit-test of the rule), plus a data-tamper variant. Since I already have `derive` recomputing everything, the data-tamper is the honest end-to-end control. I'll do the data tamper (scale baseline coarsest error by 1.3) and assert inadmissible. But note `derive` also recomputes excess etc. — no conflict.

Also E4 detail "n_mismatches: 5" — tampering the finest plateau l2_error by 1e-3 relative changed all 5 derived excess values (since excess compares base to plateau at all indices? No — tampering only the last row changes excess[-1], but the fit_order of the plateau run changes → order_move changes, and p_plateau changes; why 5 mismatches? mismatches include excess_finest, order_move, p_plateau, plus order_l2 values near the end (order_l2 has 4 entries; the last one changes by ~1e-3). So 1 (excess_finest) + 1 (order_move) + 1 (p_plateau) + maybe 2 order_l2 = 5. Fine, detected.

